            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "The Fourier transform in two dimensions: breaks a picture, a heat map or any grid of values into waves that run across it - stripes of every spacing and direction - and says how much of each there is.\n\nBroad, gentle variation sits near the centre of the (centred) spectrum; fine detail and sharp edges further out. Stripes show as bright points, at a distance from the centre set by how close the stripes are and in a direction at right angles to them. Removing the outer part blurs; removing the centre leaves only the edges - filters that are hard to build any other way. It also finds repeating patterns: the rhythm of a floor pattern, the grid of a woven texture.\n\nnp.fft.fft2 gives the full spectrum, and works on complex grids as well. For an ordinary real image np.fft.rfft2 gives the same in half the space; the full spectrum is symmetric about its centre.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.fft.fft2\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nA 2-D array (or a stack of them: the last two axes are used). Every input is transformed and sent.\n\ncentre:\nAn option, under the node. On (the default), the magnitude and phase outlets are rearranged so the steady average sits in the middle and the slowest waves around it - the way spectra are usually looked at. The spectrum outlet is never rearranged, so it always goes straight into np.fft.ifft2.\n\nnorm:\nAn option, under the node: how the result is scaled, as for the 1-D transforms.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nspectrum:\nThe complex spectrum, in numpy's order, ready for np.fft.ifft2.\n\nmagnitude:\nHow much of each wave there is.\n\nphase:\nWhere each wave starts, in radians. Sent before magnitude, which is sent before spectrum.\n\nSEEING THE MAGNITUDE:\nThe steady average and a few strong waves can be thousands of times larger than the rest; log1p of the magnitude, as in the demo, brings the faint ones into view.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\ndiagonal stripes - x + y, 4 cycles each way: sin of each position times 45 degrees (left). Its spectrum (right, through log1p) is dark except two bright points on a diagonal, four steps each way from the centre and mirrored through it: four cycles across and four down.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.fft.rfft2 is the version for real images. np.fft.ifft2 turns a spectrum back into an image. np.fft.rfft and np.fft.fft are the 1-D transforms.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
